cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or cite the manuscripts in it, please use the metadata below."
title: "Complexity Science: gated in-silico research objects for complex pathology"
type: software
version: "0.3.0"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/cloudynirvana/complexity-science"
# ORCID: add `orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"` under the author below once registered.
authors:
  - family-names: Ogbonna
    given-names: Kelechi Emeka
abstract: >-
  Computational research software and manuscripts on honest parameterisation in
  complex pathology. The CaseCard pipeline holds knowledge, evidence pointers,
  candidate mechanisms, falsifiers and qualitative Nigeria Standard Treatment
  Guidelines (NSTG) constraints behind an evidence gate that refuses to turn any
  of them into model parameters (Thesis #2). The glucose-competition package
  replaces that permanent refusal with a computable admission criterion:
  structural identifiability, a Cramer-Rao bound, and a two-sided profile
  likelihood for a declared experiment (Thesis #3). Computational research only.
  Not a medical device, not clinical decision support, not dosing advice.
keywords:
  - complexity science
  - in-silico pathology
  - identifiability
  - profile likelihood
  - evidence gate
  - tumour-immune dynamics
  - metabolic competition
  - NSTG
  - parameter smuggling
  - reproducible research
